---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import SectionHero from '../../components/SectionHero.astro'
import DocsMeta from '../../components/DocsMeta.astro'
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro'
import { withBase } from '../../lib/path'
---
<DocsLayout title="Telemetry" description="What the optional monthly report contains, how consent works, what is never collected, and where the numbers go.">
  <SectionHero section="features" />
  <h1>Telemetry</h1>
  <p>
    Tome can send one small anonymous report a month to the project, so there is a real count of
    installs and a real picture of which features get used. It is off until an admin says yes,
    and saying yes means looking at the exact report first. This page is the whole story.
  </p>
  <DocsMeta />

  <h2>How consent works</h2>
  <p>
    Admins see a card on Home, once. It has two buttons: <strong>Show the report</strong>
    and <strong>No thanks</strong>. The first opens the report Tome would send, generated from your
    instance right then by the same code that would send it, with one button underneath
    it: <strong>Send this report monthly</strong>. That button is the only way to turn telemetry on.
    There is no switch you can flip without having seen the report.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No thanks sends nothing.</strong> Not a decline, not a ping. The decision is written
      to your own database and the question is never asked again, not after updates either.</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing is sent without a stored yes.</strong> No row, a declined row and the
      environment variable below all mean the same thing: nothing leaves.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent is tied to the report's shape.</strong> If a later version changes what the
      report contains, even by one field, sending pauses and Settings asks you to look at the new
      report before it resumes.</li>
    <li><strong>You can stop any time.</strong> Settings, About, <em>Stop sending</em>. The state is
      always written out in words there, with the dates of the last and next report.</li>
    <li><strong>Every send is in your audit log</strong>, with a hash of what left, so you can check
      it against what you approved.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To hide the question entirely, for example on a compose stack you never want asked, set
    <code>TOME_TELEMETRY=false</code>. The card never shows and the setting is pinned off.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in the report</h2>
  <p>
    Every count is a bucket, every feature is a yes or no over the last 30 days, every mix is a
    share. The instance id is a random UUID your instance mints for itself; it relates to nothing
    else. This is a real example:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "schema": 1,
  "instance": "79d9d74e-07e5-40e7-a199-b6c863271a7e",

  "version": "2.6.0",
  "plugin_build": 47,
  "platform": "linux/x86_64",
  "docker": true,
  "install_age": "6-10",

  "users": "2-5",
  "readers_30d": "2-5",
  "books": "251-500",
  "libraries": "3-5",

  "book_types": { "manga": 0.55, "light_novel": 0.30, "book": 0.15 },
  "formats": { "epub": 0.55, "cbz": 0.45 },
  "languages": { "en": 0.9, "de": 0.1 },

  "reading_30d": { "sessions": "101-250", "hours": "26-50", "pages": "2501-5000", "finished": "2-5" },
  "highlights": "101-250",

  "features_30d": {
    "koreader_sync": true, "web_reader": true, "kindle_sync_code": false, "send_to_device": false,
    "bindery": true, "wishlist": true, "hardcover": true, "opds": false, "sso_users": false,
    "api_tokens": true, "reading_goals": false, "series_ratings": true, "arcs": true
  }
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Top to bottom: what runs, how big the install is, what is in it, how much it is read, which
    features are alive. <code>install_age</code> is months since the first account was created.
    <code>highlights</code> is a lifetime count because it describes what exists, not activity.
  </p>

  <h2>What is never in it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No book titles, authors, series, file names or paths.</li>
    <li>No usernames, email addresses or hostnames.</li>
    <li>No IP address, not even on the receiving side, where the access log is switched off.</li>
    <li>Nothing per user and nothing per book. Reading numbers are instance totals in buckets.</li>
    <li>No timestamps, times of day, streaks or pace. Those describe a person, not an install.</li>
    <li>No page views, clicks or sessions. The report is built from counts that already exist in
      your database, not from watching what you do.</li>
    <li>No browser-side facts like locale or theme.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Cadence</h2>
  <p>
    Once a month, plus once right after you say yes. The day drifts by a few hours of random jitter
    so a release doesn't make every instance report in the same hour. If your instance is down on
    its day, it sends on the next start, once. An instance that stops reporting leaves the public
    numbers after 90 days.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it goes</h2>
  <p>
    To <a href="https://github.com/bndct-devops/tome-pulse">tome-pulse</a>, a small public service
    that keeps the report and the month it arrived and nothing else. Reports outside the exact
    shape above are rejected, not stored. Raw rows are kept for twelve months. The aggregates it
    produces are public at <a href={withBase("/stats")}>tome.bndct.sh/stats</a>, so the people
    sending reports can see the number they helped make.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="Running a fork?">
    Point <code>TOME_TELEMETRY_URL</code> at your own receiver, or leave it at the default to count
    toward Tome's numbers. The receiver's source is the repository above.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
